1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:02,249
Greetings, aliens and earthlings!

2
00:00:02,660 --> 00:00:05,809
This is part 2 of our mini-series about the shape of the universe.

3
00:00:06,177 --> 00:00:09,317
If you didn't watch part 1, I suggest you watch it first.

4
00:00:09,677 --> 00:00:11,920
Today we will speak about curvature of space.

5
00:00:42,584 --> 00:00:49,224
To be clear, we will talk about curvature of space, not about curvature of spacetime, as examined by general relativity.

6
00:00:50,084 --> 00:00:51,154
What is curvature?

7
00:00:51,545 --> 00:00:56,411
For a surface, curvature means that the surface cannot be flattened without stretching or squeezing it.

8
00:00:56,411 --> 00:01:02,231
A bent piece of paper can be flattened, but try flattening a sphere cap, or a saddle surface: It won't work.

9
00:01:02,611 --> 00:01:03,875
That's because of the curvature.

10
00:01:04,175 --> 00:01:07,995
A sphere is said to have positive curvature, aka spherical curvature.

11
00:01:08,275 --> 00:01:11,995
A saddle surface has negative curvature, aka hyperbolic curvature.

12
00:01:12,255 --> 00:01:15,273
A surface can be differently curved at different locations.

13
00:01:15,694 --> 00:01:20,529
A banana for example is positively curved on one side, and negatively on the other side.

14
00:01:20,529 --> 00:01:26,769
Now, the difference is easily visible "from the outside", but how to explain it to flatsnails inside the surface?

15
00:01:27,209 --> 00:01:29,649
Well, you could try to explain it with triangles.

16
00:01:30,169 --> 00:01:35,889
On a flat plane, the angles of a triangle sum up to 180°, no matter its size.

17
00:01:36,209 --> 00:01:37,429
Why is this so?

18
00:01:37,729 --> 00:01:40,189
Well, imagine a flatsnail following the border.

19
00:01:41,029 --> 00:01:42,789
Be patient, it's a snail.

20
00:01:44,769 --> 00:01:46,109
This might take a while.

21
00:01:46,949 --> 00:01:47,889
You know what?

22
00:01:48,169 --> 00:01:48,989
Forget about the flatsnail.

23
00:01:49,929 --> 00:01:51,080
Let's take a shark!

24
00:01:51,341 --> 00:01:51,834
On a lightcycle!

25
00:01:52,677 --> 00:01:54,717
With a fricking laser attached to his head!

26
00:01:55,097 --> 00:01:56,377
Now, that's better.

27
00:01:56,697 --> 00:01:59,509
Each time the shark takes a corner, the laser goes "woosh".

28
00:02:02,377 --> 00:02:07,657
And afterwards, he has made a full turn - so, the sums of the "wooshs" sum up to a full turn.

29
00:02:07,977 --> 00:02:11,017
But each "woosh" sums up with one angle to 180°.

30
00:02:11,977 --> 00:02:15,397
From this you can easily calculate that the sum of the angles is 180°.

31
00:02:16,677 --> 00:02:19,008
Now, let's look what happens on a sphere.

32
00:02:19,253 --> 00:02:25,693
This triangle, for example, has three 90° angles. 270° in total.

33
00:02:25,693 --> 00:02:28,233
Where do the additional 90° come from?

34
00:02:28,553 --> 00:02:29,735
The answer: Curvature.

35
00:02:30,232 --> 00:02:33,764
The sphere has positive curvature, which somehow inflates the angles.

36
00:02:34,274 --> 00:02:39,128
Small triangles behave almost as on a plane, but the bigger they are, the more the angles grow.

37
00:02:39,591 --> 00:02:44,926
Think of curvature as overpressure: It gives the triangles a cushion form and makes the angles less pointy.

38
00:02:45,169 --> 00:02:48,169
But what about a negatively curved surface - a saddle surface?

39
00:02:48,649 --> 00:02:52,579
Here, the opposite happens: The angles are inferior to what you might expect.

40
00:02:52,579 --> 00:02:58,324
Think of negative curvature as underpressure which gives the triangles an evacuated shape and makes corners pointier.

41
00:02:58,646 --> 00:03:04,666
By the way, the same happens to arbitrary polygons: Negative curvature sucks them out, positive pumps them up.

42
00:03:05,046 --> 00:03:07,097
But where does this strange behaviour come from?

43
00:03:07,282 --> 00:03:11,790
You might get an idea when you try to put a round patch of fabric onto a positively curved surface.

44
00:03:12,379 --> 00:03:17,838
You'll notice that near the border, there is too much fabric - you have to hide fabric triangles by folding and sewing.

45
00:03:17,838 --> 00:03:20,672
Tailors call the hidden triangles "darts".

46
00:03:21,080 --> 00:03:26,780
So, what happens here is that in the case of positive curvature, the border of disks is shorter than you might expect.

47
00:03:27,080 --> 00:03:31,160
In other words, the outskirts of any region are smaller than you might expect.

48
00:03:31,424 --> 00:03:34,244
There is surface that has been "sewn away", so to say.

49
00:03:34,544 --> 00:03:43,415
In consequence, when you connect three or more points with shortest lines, the lines have the tendency to bulge outwards, in order to benefit from the shorter distances in the outskirts.

50
00:03:43,415 --> 00:03:52,735
It's like intercontinental flights on Earth: The flight paths have the tendency to bulge towards the poles, because near a pole, the distance between lines of longitude is shorter.

51
00:03:53,155 --> 00:03:56,904
That's the reason why polygons have a cushion form on positively curved surfaces.

52
00:03:57,457 --> 00:04:00,801
Another thing that happens on a positively curved surface is that parallels converge.

53
00:04:01,745 --> 00:04:05,352
The reason is, so to speak, the fabric between the parallels is "sewn away".

54
00:04:05,815 --> 00:04:08,109
Now, what about negatively curved surfaces?

55
00:04:08,109 --> 00:04:11,569
Let's try to put a round patch of fabric onto a saddle surface.

56
00:04:12,049 --> 00:04:18,309
We notice that it doesn't work, unless we tear the border apart, or insert fabric triangles to adapt to the shape.

57
00:04:18,729 --> 00:04:24,229
So, in this case, we have the inverse effect: Far from the center we have more surface than you might expect.

58
00:04:24,709 --> 00:04:31,136
In consequence, shortest connections between corners avoid the outskirts and take a shortcut through the center where distances are smaller.

59
00:04:31,136 --> 00:04:35,152
That's why the edges bulge inwards, and the corners are pointier than on a plane.

60
00:04:35,578 --> 00:04:42,193
For example, if earth were a hyperboloid, you would see flight paths bending towards the equator, because there distances are shorter.

61
00:04:42,516 --> 00:04:45,616
What happens to parallel lines on a negatively curved surface?

62
00:04:46,082 --> 00:04:47,602
Well, they diverge.

63
00:04:47,942 --> 00:04:51,807
That happens, so to speak, because of the additional fabric inserted between them.

64
00:04:51,807 --> 00:04:54,827
Now, can we do the same in three-dimensional space?

65
00:04:55,167 --> 00:04:56,327
Of course we can.

66
00:04:56,567 --> 00:04:59,918
The three-dimensional equivalent to a round patch is a spherical ball.

67
00:05:00,201 --> 00:05:03,921
Now, in flat space, a ball behaves exactly how it should.

68
00:05:04,221 --> 00:05:07,969
But in a positively curved universe, the surface of a big ball is smaller.

69
00:05:08,317 --> 00:05:16,911
In other words, outer space is smaller than you think - as if someone had stolen whole wedges of space and bridged the rest with portals, hoping nobody would notice.

70
00:05:16,911 --> 00:05:23,056
In a negatively curved universe, the surface of a ball is bigger, there is more outer space than you might think.

71
00:05:23,398 --> 00:05:25,561
As if someone had inserted additional space.

72
00:05:25,949 --> 00:05:31,510
The three-dimensional analogon to polygons are polyhedra - and they react to curvature in the same way.

73
00:05:31,812 --> 00:05:38,686
When you take a polyhedron and put it into positively curved space, the edges and faces bulge outwards to benefit from the shrunken outer space.

74
00:05:38,927 --> 00:05:41,774
In negatively curved space, the opposite happens.

75
00:05:41,774 --> 00:05:49,154
Here, outer space is spacier than you think, so the edges and faces bulge inwards to avoid those long outer-space distances.

76
00:05:49,554 --> 00:05:55,731
You may think of positive curvature as overpressure which blows up polygons to a cushion form and makes corners more obtuse.

77
00:05:56,173 --> 00:06:00,678
Negative curvature is like underpressure which gives the polygon an evacuated shape with pointy corners.

78
00:06:01,023 --> 00:06:03,013
But is our universe flat or curved?

79
00:06:03,013 --> 00:06:09,006
To find that out, we would have to measure really, really big triangles or polyhedra, and that's not easy.

80
00:06:09,246 --> 00:06:11,246
We will come to this point next episode.

81
00:06:11,646 --> 00:06:15,860
Our goal today is to understand what curvature is, and to see some examples.

82
00:06:16,343 --> 00:06:18,414
The universe could even have mixed curvature.

83
00:06:18,911 --> 00:06:19,813
Remember the banana?

84
00:06:20,253 --> 00:06:23,568
Its curvature is positive on one side, negative on the other one.

85
00:06:23,954 --> 00:06:26,336
Couldn't the universe be the same - some kind of hyperbanana?

86
00:06:27,218 --> 00:06:28,555
Well, yes and no.

87
00:06:28,555 --> 00:06:32,483
It could be, but that would violate something called the "cosmological principle".

88
00:06:32,964 --> 00:06:36,984
That's the assumption that on a large scale, the universe looks everywhere the same.

89
00:06:37,388 --> 00:06:41,937
It's not a patchwork of different zones with different properties, shapes and laws of physics.

90
00:06:42,539 --> 00:06:48,949
This excludes "mixed curvature", just as it excludes zones with different physical constants, and just as it excludes an edge.

91
00:06:49,131 --> 00:06:57,967
This assumption is a consequence of what earthlings call "Ockham's Razor", the principle that simple hypotheses are better than complicated ones, if both are equally supported by evidence.

92
00:06:57,967 --> 00:07:04,047
Think of Ockham's Razor as a proven search order for hypotheses: It's useful to check the simplest hypotheses first.

93
00:07:04,367 --> 00:07:13,624
For example, when I see a crop circle, I could hypothesize a conspiracy of local farm boys who spent a whole night in a field, trampling crops down with primitive earthling tools.

94
00:07:14,125 --> 00:07:19,196
But a much simpler hypothesis would be a fellow alien with a cropwriter who wanted to contact a ship in orbit.

95
00:07:19,837 --> 00:07:21,429
Hitchhikers do this all the time.

96
00:07:21,847 --> 00:07:25,796
So, there is no need for a conspiracy theory if there is a simpler explanation.

97
00:07:25,796 --> 00:07:33,476
So, from now on, let's assume that the universe is either flat, or entirely positively curved, or entirely negatively curved.

98
00:07:34,016 --> 00:07:35,601
So, no hyperbanana.

99
00:07:35,855 --> 00:07:38,227
The simplest positively curved model is the hypersphere.

100
00:07:38,908 --> 00:07:44,302
Before we can understand the hypersphere, let's try to explain to Flatsnails how you can construct a sphere.

101
00:07:44,686 --> 00:07:45,986
How would you do that?

102
00:07:46,186 --> 00:07:49,375
Maybe we could start with two squares, one per hemisphere.

103
00:07:49,375 --> 00:07:53,573
We pump them up with curvature until the 90° angles are smoothed out.

104
00:07:54,077 --> 00:07:57,377
Now, each part has a smooth, straight border without corners.

105
00:07:57,937 --> 00:08:00,992
We might call this a zero-gon, but the usual term is "equator".

106
00:08:01,559 --> 00:08:04,547
Then we glue the two together along the equator, with portals.

107
00:08:05,032 --> 00:08:08,224
That's probably where one of the Flatsnails would ask why we need the curvature.

108
00:08:08,691 --> 00:08:12,611
Can't we simply take two squares, portalize them together, and it's done?

109
00:08:13,031 --> 00:08:14,411
Wouldn't that be much easier?

110
00:08:14,851 --> 00:08:17,090
Easier maybe, but there is a problem.

111
00:08:17,090 --> 00:08:22,070
See, dear flatsnails, each corner point is only adjacent to two square corners.

112
00:08:22,450 --> 00:08:25,762
But you can't glue two 90° corners together in a smooth manner.

113
00:08:26,146 --> 00:08:31,193
So, you have to use curvature to pump the angles up to 180° to make a smooth gluing.

114
00:08:31,456 --> 00:08:39,716
The two "pumped up" squares would be somewhat like a disk and somewhat like a square: They would have no corners, like a disk, and have straight edges, like a square.

115
00:08:40,196 --> 00:08:42,706
"Straight" means straight for creatures inside the surface.

116
00:08:43,196 --> 00:08:45,250
That would probably blow the mind of some flatsnails.

117
00:08:46,884 --> 00:08:50,544
But for you, it would be easy because you could secretly think of hemispheres.

118
00:08:50,884 --> 00:09:01,104
But now, we can do the same thing in three-dimensional space: We take two cubes, pump them up with positive curvature to flatten out the corners and edges, and glue them together with portals.

119
00:09:01,104 --> 00:09:08,404
Those pumped up cubes would be somewhat like cubes and somewhat like spheres: Edges and corners smoothed out, but flat sides.

120
00:09:08,704 --> 00:09:10,612
The result is called a hypersphere.

121
00:09:10,861 --> 00:09:16,488
In the last episode we have seen that when we take one cube and glue opposing faces together, we get a hypertorus.

122
00:09:16,488 --> 00:09:20,988
Why do we need positive curvature for the hypersphere, but not for the hypertorus?

123
00:09:21,308 --> 00:09:22,828
Well, because of the corners.

124
00:09:23,368 --> 00:09:30,930
Look, in the hypertorus, this corner is identical with this one, and this one, and - actually, all corners are one and the same.

125
00:09:30,930 --> 00:09:36,951
So, around this corner, we have eight parts of the space matching perfectly together - no need for curvature.

126
00:09:37,415 --> 00:09:43,315
On the other hand, let's look what happens when we glue two cubes together to a hypersphere, but forget the curvature.

127
00:09:43,655 --> 00:09:47,195
This corner is identical with this one, and... that's it.

128
00:09:47,195 --> 00:09:52,371
So, the vicinity of this corner are two parts of the two cubes - that doesn't match together.

129
00:09:52,612 --> 00:09:57,532
We have to blow up the cubes such that the corners get smoothed out - then the gluing works.

130
00:09:57,832 --> 00:10:06,595
As mentioned before, the surfaces of the hemispheres are flat - they only look curved in our 3D representation, because we can't display the curvature of the space inside.

131
00:10:07,115 --> 00:10:09,240
That's why we can glue the two balls together.

132
00:10:09,520 --> 00:10:13,385
That gluing surface is, so to say, the equator of the hypersphere.

133
00:10:13,385 --> 00:10:16,885
Let's make a journey through a hypersphere universe at super-light speed.

134
00:10:17,485 --> 00:10:22,485
Actually, there is only one thing capable of travelling faster than light without warping space: A rumor.

135
00:10:23,205 --> 00:10:29,999
So, let's invent a rumor - say, the rumor that Youtube celebrity CGP Grey is actually a superintelligent stick figure.

136
00:10:30,201 --> 00:10:33,172
The rumor bubble expands from earth at super-light speed.

137
00:10:33,766 --> 00:10:40,091
First, the bubble surface grows like you would expect, but after a while, it grows slower and becomes less and less convex.

138
00:10:40,695 --> 00:10:44,975
Eventually, the surface flips and becomes concave - we have crossed the cosmic equator.

139
00:10:45,455 --> 00:10:47,775
The bubble is flipped inside out and shrinks again.

140
00:10:48,295 --> 00:10:54,015
Eventually, it becomes smaller and smaller until it vanishes in one point - the antipodes of our starting point.

141
00:10:54,535 --> 00:10:58,957
So, we have constructed our first positively curved space: The hypersphere.

142
00:10:59,160 --> 00:11:01,580
But this is far from being the only curved model.

143
00:11:02,360 --> 00:11:11,259
In the last episode, I mentioned that when you take a square and add two pairs of flipped portals such that opposing points are glued together, you get something called projective plane.

144
00:11:11,700 --> 00:11:16,369
What I didn't mention was that like for the sphere, you need curvature to have smooth gluing at the corners.

145
00:11:16,369 --> 00:11:23,466
The projective plane is a strange and interesting place where parallels intersect in infinity and all conic sections are basically the same.

146
00:11:23,930 --> 00:11:27,592
Also, it's non-orientable because it contains a moebius strip.

147
00:11:28,118 --> 00:11:30,246
Maybe I'll make a video about this one day.

148
00:11:30,512 --> 00:11:31,932
But not anytime soon.

149
00:11:32,232 --> 00:11:34,472
You can do the same thing in three dimensions.

150
00:11:35,092 --> 00:11:42,652
You take one cube, pump it up with positive curvature like you did for the hypersphere, and then add portals to glue every point to its antipodal point.

151
00:11:42,652 --> 00:11:45,777
The result is what topologists call "projective space".

152
00:11:46,186 --> 00:11:49,326
Unlike its two-dimensional counterpart, it's orientable.

153
00:11:50,106 --> 00:11:52,186
Now, you don't need to start with a cube.

154
00:11:52,686 --> 00:11:56,586
Take, for example, a dodecahedron and glue opposing faces together.

155
00:11:59,586 --> 00:12:06,590
Opposing pentagons are oriented differently, so you have to rotate them in some way - let's say, a 180° turn.

156
00:12:06,970 --> 00:12:08,276
What space is this?

157
00:12:08,276 --> 00:12:13,831
Well, when we pump the result a little up, we get again the hyper-hemisphere with opposite points glued together.

158
00:12:14,296 --> 00:12:17,665
So, this is just another way of constructing the projective space.

159
00:12:18,069 --> 00:12:21,329
But what if we do only a 36° turn for each pair?

160
00:12:21,569 --> 00:12:23,728
We get a far more interesting space.

161
00:12:24,201 --> 00:12:27,032
Now, each corner corresponds to 3 other corners.

162
00:12:27,356 --> 00:12:33,743
When we look what happens around one corner, we will notice that the puzzle almost matches together, the corners are only a bit too pointy.

163
00:12:33,743 --> 00:12:38,023
So, we have to add just a little bit of positive curvature, to close the gaps.

164
00:12:38,463 --> 00:12:42,574
This positively curved space is called the Poincaré dodecahedral space.

165
00:12:42,898 --> 00:12:47,429
It has been proposed in 2003 as possible shape of the universe by an earthling called Jean-Pierre Luminet.

166
00:12:48,724 --> 00:12:50,884
We will come back to this in Episode 3.

167
00:12:51,584 --> 00:12:56,284
What happens when we turn the portals not by a 1/10 turn, but by a 3/10 turn?

168
00:12:56,764 --> 00:13:01,404
Well, that changes quite a bit: Now, all corners come together to a single point!

169
00:13:01,624 --> 00:13:08,724
In consequence, the environment of this point becomes quite crowded, we must drastically reduce the corner angles to make place for everybody.

170
00:13:09,024 --> 00:13:13,181
In other words, we need a lot of negative curvature to make the corners match.

171
00:13:13,181 --> 00:13:18,641
This is called the Seifert-Weber space, and it's an example for a negatively curved finite model.

172
00:13:18,921 --> 00:13:21,986
Another example for a negatively curved model would be the Picard horn.

173
00:13:22,463 --> 00:13:33,963
It's difficult to explain without referring to higher mathematics, so I'll only sketch the construction of its two-dimensional analogon: That would be a horn, where opposing points of the edge are glued together - when you leave here, you enter here.

174
00:13:34,143 --> 00:13:38,582
The pointy end of the horn is infinitely long, but so narrow that the volume is finite.

175
00:13:38,582 --> 00:13:44,871
In this video, we have seen three positively curved and two negatively curved models for a finite universe without edge.

176
00:13:45,254 --> 00:13:51,656
Of course, I never said that the universe is necessarily finite, I only said it's a possibility - it could very well be infinite.

177
00:13:51,959 --> 00:13:55,213
There are many other models, finite ones and infinite ones.

178
00:13:55,415 --> 00:13:57,215
But I think we had enough for today.

179
00:13:57,475 --> 00:14:04,043
The objective of this episode wasn't to provide an exhaustive list of models, but to give you an idea of what "curved space" actually means.

180
00:14:04,043 --> 00:14:12,663
In the third and last episode, we will speak about the expansion of the universe, and discuss hypotheses and evidence concerning the actual shape of the universe.

181
00:14:13,083 --> 00:14:16,243
One personal note: I am more of a mathematician than of a physicist.

182
00:14:16,923 --> 00:14:19,763
So, I can't promise I won't make any errors in physics.

183
00:14:20,323 --> 00:14:24,449
To give an example, last time I told you that an Alice universe would turn matter into antimatter.

184
00:14:26,266 --> 00:14:29,882
But as one viewer noted, things might not be as simple as that.

185
00:14:30,349 --> 00:14:36,333
By the way, this comment wasn't on YouTube, but on the site Universe Today which posted an article on the first episode.

186
00:14:36,636 --> 00:14:39,689
This article brought me a lot of views, thanks for that!

187
00:14:39,913 --> 00:14:43,719
If you like astronomy, you will probably like this site - give it a try!

188
00:14:43,719 --> 00:14:55,459
As for my next videos: When I asked last time whether you would prefer me to alternate with "Earthlings 101" or finish this series first, about two thirds of you voted for finishing this series, and one third for alternating.

189
00:14:55,959 --> 00:14:57,959
That's why you are watching this video today.

190
00:14:58,419 --> 00:15:06,879
But that's also why I've decided to choose a hybrid solution: After this video, I will program the next episode of Earthlings 101, and afterwards finish this series.

191
00:15:07,339 --> 00:15:10,979
This way I don't scare off viewers who don't care at all about cosmotopology.

192
00:15:11,999 --> 00:15:13,139
That's all for today.

193
00:15:13,819 --> 00:15:18,202
Like, subscribe, tell your mum, and as always: Don't forget to be alien!

